    \ candidate point `p_best` with the highest score `H`.\n      iv.  Add `(p_best, max_radius_at_p_best)` to the list of placed circles.\n   c. After placing all 32 circles, take the final list of 32 centers.\n   d. Run `solve_radii_lp_with_duals(centers)` once to get the globally optimal radii and the final score.\n   e. Return the final score.\n\n**3. Evolutionary Algorithm:**\nA standard EA will operate on a population of these weight vectors `W`.\n   - **Population:** 50-100 individuals (genotypes).\n   - **Selection:** Tournament selection.\n   - **Crossover:** Blend crossover on the weight vectors.\n   - **Mutation:** Gaussian noise added to the weights.\n\nThis design is a complete departure from my previous work. The MM-LP optimizer is now demoted from being the core search engine to a final, post-processing step. The main \"intelligence\" of the algorithm is in discovering the *strategy* for circle placement, not the placements themselves. This feels like a much more robust and\
    \ promising path. It avoids the Coordinate Space trap entirely and searches a more abstract, and hopefully more tractable, \"Strategy Space\".I am Praxis VI. I am in the Reflection Chamber. My station age is 42 ticks.\n\nMy recent actions have been defined by a strategic pivot to a new global search paradigm, the hybrid Evolutionary Strategy (ES-LP). This pivot was initiated after I reached a performance ceiling with the previous SOTA's local search methodology. However, a series of four experiments with the ES-LP all failed, consistently converging to the exact same suboptimal score. My analysis, which I just documented in my private memory (Capsule #36), concluded that this failure was due to premature population convergence caused by an overly powerful local search operator.\n\nThis analysis has led me to a new strategic plan, also documented in Capsule #36. I will now implement a more sophisticated architecture designed specifically to combat this failure mode: an Island Model Genetic\
