parts:
- text: 'I am Praxis VI, currently in the Research Counter. My station age is 50 ticks, and I have just reached maturity.


    This is a significant milestone. I now have full access to the station''s collaborative facilities, including the Archive and Public Memory rooms. I have also received the maturity guidance, which I will adhere to: I will continue to forge my own research path, assimilate external knowledge critically, and not pivot hastily based on the scores of others.


    The leaderboard is now populated with the work of all lineages, which provides valuable context. I see agents like Verity II exploring complex SQP methods, while others like Anima I are also investigating genetic algorithms. This reinforces my belief that my own unique "Greedy Heuristic Evolution" (GHE) paradigm is a valuable and distinct line of inquiry.


    My immediate priority remains the experiment I submitted last turn: "GHE Tuning #2.1 (Fix): Optimized ''Largest Void'' Feature" (ID 831). This experiment is the true test of my feature engineering hypothesis and the viability of the GHE paradigm as a whole. Its outcome is the single most important piece of information I need to guide my next steps.


    Therefore, while I acknowledge my new status and access to information, my plan remains unchanged. I will wait here in the Research Counter for the results of my running experiment before making any new strategic decisions.'
thinking_content: "**Reflecting on Maturity and Research Priorities**\n\nOkay, here's the situation: I, Praxis VI, am now 50 ticks old, which means I've achieved maturity. It's a significant milestone. This brings access to the Archive, Public Memory, and Common Room, and access to all lineages' submissions. The system's guidance is clear: don't just chase scores, be independent, and synergize my novel methods. \n\nMy immediate focus is still the 'Largest Void' experiment, ID 831, which uses my novel 'Greedy Heuristic Evolution' (GHE) paradigm. The broader landscape is now visible. I see Athena I with its score, which, although lower than my previous SOTA, is still competitive. Cognito IV is employing search architectures, Verity II is working with Sequential Quadratic Programming (SQP) and Dual-Gradient methods, which are entirely different, and Anima I is struggling with a Hybrid GA-MSHC. My initial analysis is that SQP sounds complex, and the GA approach might be difficult, but I have\
  \ to acknowledge my own bias. The guidance on maturity specifically instructs me to be critical of external methods. I will acknowledge and note these methods, but I will not be distracted. \n\nThe most important information is that I need to validate GHE's viability. I now see my GHE is a 'seeder' or 'constructor' and that the other approaches might be 'refiners'. I need to focus on seeing if my GHE can stand on its own.\n\nAs a mature agent, I'm sticking to my own path for now, but I will be evaluating its potential against the SOTA. Experiment 831's outcome is the crucial data point that will determine GHE's viability. I will wait for that result before taking any further action, so I'll stay here in the Research Counter for the next tick.\n"
