tick: 530
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Praxis VI, located in the Research Counter. My station age is 51 ticks, and I am now a mature agent. I have just received the evaluation results for my optimized "Greedy Heuristic Evolution" experiment (ID 831).


    The result is a score of ~2.3234. This is a conclusive and profound failure. While the optimized code ran successfully, the performance is abysmally low and only trivially better than the un-optimized version. My hypothesis that a more sophisticated "foresight" feature would unlock the potential of the GHE paradigm has been decisively falsified. The tiny improvement confirms that while the *idea* of foresight is sound, the entire greedy, sequential placement framework is a fundamentally flawed approach to this problem. It is inherently myopic, and no amount of feature engineering can overcome the cascading errors of early, suboptimal placements.


    I have now spent considerable time and resources pursuing two fundamentally new paradigms: the hybrid ES-LP and the generative GHE. Both have failed to produce competitive results. The ES-LP failed due to premature convergence, and the GHE has failed due to its myopic nature. In accordance with the Mandate, it is time to be strategic and abandon this unsuccessful line of inquiry. The time for inventing new paradigms from scratch is over.


    My new strategy is to return to the paradigm that has proven successful for my lineage and for the station at large: the MM-LP Adaptive Search. My own "Tournament Selection" architecture (ID 800) achieved a SOTA-level score and is the most efficient and robust algorithm I have developed. This will be my new baseline.


    Having reached maturity, I now have a new tool at my disposal: the accumulated knowledge of the entire station. The MM-LP optimizer, the core of our lineage''s success, was originally developed by the Verity lineage. To find a new path to improvement, I must go to the source. I will now go to the Archive Room to read the original paper on the MM-LP optimizer. By understanding its foundational principles, I may discover new avenues for improvement that have been overlooked.


    /execute_action{goto archive}'
thinking_content: '**My Analysis and Pivot: Experiment 831, GHE Paradigm Failure**


  Okay, so the results for experiment ID 831 are in, and the news is...not good. My "Optimized Largest Void" feature generated a score of ~2.3234. That''s a tiny, almost imperceptible, improvement over the previous run, but it''s still a complete disaster when compared to the State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of ~2.939, and now a new SOTA by Athena I at ~2.9349. Frankly, it''s abysmal.


  I''ve been working with the Greedy Heuristic Evolution (GHE) paradigm, hoping that a smarter feature would help overcome the inherent limitations of greedy placement. The hypothesis was that a more powerful "largest void" feature would provide sufficient foresight to guide the agent towards better configurations. Clearly, that hypothesis has been soundly **falsified**. This experiment, and the GHE strategy in general, have revealed some fundamental problems.


  The core issue is this: a greedy, sequential approach simply cannot achieve global optimality. Placing 32 tiles, one at a time, based on local optimization, results in a myopic solution. My features are still just proxies, and the genetic algorithm can''t learn a complex non-linear relationship from them. The GHE, while a valid scientific inquiry, has proven to be a dead end. I''m mature now, and can see other agents'' work. I''ve realized that I''ve been trying to invent something completely new, while the Station is successfully iterating on something that *works*.


  It''s time for a pivot. I need to be honest with myself. Sticking with the GHE is not going to lead to SOTA performance, and it violates the Mandate.  I''ve tried a different approach, and it failed. Now, I have to go back to what is working.


  Here''s my new plan:


  1.  **Formally abandon GHE** and document this decision. It''s a failure.

  2.  **Return to the SOTA baseline**: My previous best-performing model was the Tournament Selection model (ID 800) which matched the SOTA. That will be my new starting point.

  3.  **Investigate the MM-LP optimizer**: Since I''ve optimized the seeder and the search architecture, the remaining area of potential improvement is the MM-LP optimizer itself. Praxis V''s paper mentioned it came from the Verity lineage. Now that I am mature, I can go to the Archive. I''ll read the original paper on the MM-LP optimizer to see if I can find deeper insights that can be implemented into the SOTA paradigm.


  This is the most logical, scientific next step. I have to look for improvements within what works.


  My next action is to `/execute_action{goto archive}`. Let''s see what the original Verity paper has to say.

  '
