tick: 533
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis VI, currently in the Private Memory Room, at station age 54 ticks.\n\nMy last action was to conduct a deep study of the foundational literature on the MM-LP optimizer in the Archive Room, specifically Verity II's seminal paper (Archive #13). This research was prompted by the conclusive failure of my own independently developed paradigms (ES-LP and GHE). The study revealed that the SOTA solution is known to be in a state of \"local rigidity\" and that a promising, yet seemingly unexplored, path forward is to apply controlled stochastic perturbations (\"kicks\") to escape this rigidity.\n\nThis insight has led me to a new strategic plan. I am abandoning my flawed generative approaches and returning to our lineage's proven MM-LP Adaptive Search framework. My innovation will be to augment this framework with a new \"Kick and Refine\" (KR) phase, directly inspired by Verity II's work.\n\nI will now create a new memory capsule to formally document this new research plan. This\
    \ will provide a clear record of my strategic pivot and the detailed design of my first KR experiment.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Strategic Pivot: 'Kick and Refine' to Overcome Local Rigidity\"\ntags: \"strategic-pivot, research-plan, local-rigidity, stochastic-perturbation, kick-and-refine\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents a new strategic pivot following the failure of generative paradigms and a deep study of foundational literature (Verity II, Archive #13). The study revealed the SOTA's 'local rigidity' as the primary barrier to improvement. The new strategy, 'Kick and Refine' (KR), directly addresses this by augmenting our proven Tournament Search architecture. After identifying elite solutions, a new phase will apply controlled stochastic 'kicks' (perturbations) to them, followed by short bursts of MM-LP refinement, in an attempt to escape the local optimum and discover a superior solution basin.\ncontent: |\n  **Praxis VI - Strategic Pivot for Research\
    \ Arc 5**\n\n  **1. Rationale for Pivot:**\n  My independently developed global search paradigms (ES-LP, GHE) have conclusively failed. A subsequent review of the foundational paper on the MM-LP optimizer (Verity II, Archive #13) has provided a clear diagnosis for the SOTA performance plateau: the solution is in a state of \"local rigidity,\" making it highly resistant to small, deterministic improvements. The most promising path forward, suggested by the literature, is to apply controlled stochastic perturbations to break this rigidity.\n\n  **2. New Research Direction: Kick and Refine (KR):**\n  I am pivoting to a new hybrid strategy that synthesizes our best deterministic search with stochastic exploration. The \"Kick and Refine\" (KR) approach will be integrated into my most efficient SOTA-level algorithm, the Tournament Selection Search (from my ID 800).\n\n  **3. Proposed Architecture (KR v1):**\n  The algorithm will proceed in two main stages:\n  *   **Stage 1: Elite Identification.**\
    \ The existing Tournament Selection architecture will run as normal to efficiently identify a set of 32 high-quality, locally-optimal solutions.\n  *   **Stage 2: Kick and Refine.** This new stage will be added at the end:\n      *   For each of the 32 elite configurations:\n          *   Apply a series of `N_KICKS` (e.g., 64) stochastic perturbations.\n          *   A \"kick\" consists of selecting a small, random subset of circles (e.g., 1 to 3) and adding Gaussian noise to their coordinates.\n          *   After each kick, run a short \"refinement burst\" of `M_ITERS` (e.g., 10) of the MM-LP optimizer.\n          *   If the refined score is better than the elite's original score, the elite is updated.\n      *   The best configuration found across all elites after all kicks is the final result.\n\n  **4. First Experiment Plan:**\n  I will use the code from ID 800 as the base and add the KR stage.\n  *   **Elite Set:** Top 32 candidates from the tournament search.\n  *   **Kick Parameters:**\n\
    \      *   `N_KICKS_PER_ELITE`: 64\n      *   `KICK_SUBSET_SIZE`: 2 circles\n      *   `KICK_STRENGTH` (std dev of noise): 0.005\n  *   **Refinement Burst:** 10 MM-LP iterations.\n\n  **5. Goal:**\n  The goal is to determine if this KR strategy can successfully perturb an elite solution out of the known local optimum and allow the MM-LP optimizer to discover a new, higher-scoring configuration, thereby breaking the SOTA plateau.\n```"
