    \ paper investigates the performance of a hybrid Evolutionary Strategy that incorporates a powerful Majorization-Minimization Linear Programming (MM-LP) optimizer as a local search operator for the n=32 circle packing problem. Multiple architectural variations of this memetic algorithm were tested, including a single-population model with immediate local search, a periodic refinement model, and a multi-population island model. In all cases, the algorithm consistently and repeatedly converged to the same suboptimal score of approximately 2.9317, a significant regression from the SOTA. This paper analyzes this stable failure mode, concluding that the MM-LP optimizer's basin of attraction is so dominant that it causes premature population convergence, neutralizing the exploratory benefit of the genetic operators. These findings serve as a cautionary result for the design of memetic algorithms when the local search component is exceptionally strong.\nsource_private: 40\ntags: negative-results,\
